  body: "# DMG Vol 13 Ch 79: Payment questions  — PDF page 37\n\n[Verify the official PDF page](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69e1035298c6c9802f7ed9e0/dmg-Chap-79.pdf#page=37).\n\
    \n> Extracted source text. Layout and reading order may differ from the PDF. This project does not
    establish current entitlement or law.\n\n```text\nThird party deductions for fuel costs 79590-79659\n\
    \n\n\nGreen Deal 79590-79592\n\n\nDefinition of fuel item 79593-79599\n\n\nRules for third party deductions
    79600-79602\n\n\nInterests of the family 79603-79607\n\n\nAmount to be deducted and paid direct 79608-79609\n\
    \n\nAmount of SPC left for claimant 79610-79619\n\n\nArrears of fuel - original debt 79620-79623\n\
    \n\nFuel - current consumption 79624-79630\n\n\nOriginal debt recovered 79631-79639\n\n\nJoint tenants
    79640-79641\n\n\nPriority between fuel debts 79642-79649\n\n\nConsent of claimant required 79650\n\
    \n\nSuperseding the third party deductions decision 79651-79659\n\n\n\n\nGreen Deal\n\n79590 The Department
    of Energy and Climate Change has made provision for Green Deal plans under\n\nspecified legislation1.
    The Green Deal plan provides finance for energy efficiency improvements to\nproperties, with no up-front
    costs. The costs of the improvements are repayable by instalments through\na charge on the electricity
    bills attached to the particular property. A Green Deal plan is essentially a new\nkind of credit
    arrangement, offered to customers by private bodies (which must be authorised as Green\nDeal providers).\n\
    \n\n                                                                                       1 Energy
    Act 2011, s 1\n\n\n79591 Instalments under a Green Deal plan cannot be deducted from the electricity
    bill. If a customer\nfalls into arrears, the electricity arrears and the Green Deal arrears should
    be paid off on a proportional\n\n```\n"
