- '@id': https://chris-page-gov.github.io/okf-dwp/id/page/86/0031
  '@type': schema:DigitalDocument
  id: page/86/0031
  route: page/86/0031
  title: Chapter 86 — PDF page 31
  type: Source PDF page
  description: Single status payments 86092 A payment which is made to a person to redress past pay
    inequalities is a payment of earnings 1 and may have to be taken into account if that person is
    still working for that employer. These payments are sometim
  body: "# DMG Vol 14 Ch 86: Earnings  — PDF page 31\n\n[Verify the official PDF page](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/698462dd468d351e1406b4a7/dmgch86.pdf#page=31).\n\
    \n> Extracted source text. Layout and reading order may differ from the PDF. This project does not
    establish current entitlement or law.\n\n```text\nSingle status payments\n\n86092 A payment which
    is made to a person to redress past pay inequalities is a payment of earnings 1\nand may have to be
    taken into account if that person is still working for that employer. These payments\nare sometimes
    called “single status payments” but may be called something else.\n\n\n                          \
    \                       1 Minter v. Kingston Upon Hull City Council [2011] Civ 1155\n\n\n\nSpecial
    occupations\n\n86093 Some occupations are known as special occupations. These are\n\n\n1. auxiliary
    coastguards for coastal rescue activities\n\n\n2. in Scotland a P/T fire fighter employed by the Scottish
    Fire and Rescue Service 1\n\n\n3. in England and Wales, a P/T fire fighter employed by a fire and
    rescue authority under relevant\n\nlegislation2\n\n\n4. P/T work manning or launching a lifeboat\n\
    \n\n5. members of the territorial or reserve forces3 .\n\n\n                                    1
    Fire (Scotland) Act 2005, s 1A; 2 Fire and Rescue Services Act 2004;\n                           \
    \                                   3 SS (Contributions) Regs 2001, Sch 6, Part 1\n\n\nAuxiliary coastguards\n\
    86094 Payments received for watch keeping duties should be treated as earnings. Payments for\nexpenses
    of coastal rescue activities should also be treated as earnings, unless they were wholly,\nexclusively
    and necessarily incurred in the performance of the coastguard's duties.\n\n\nPart-time members of
    a fire brigade\n86095 Payments for drills, services or retaining fees, should be treated as earnings.
    Payments for\nexpenses should also be treated as earnings, if they were not wholly, exclusively and
    necessarily incurred\nin the performance of the duties.\n\n\nPart-time manning or launching of a lifeboat\n\
    86096 Treat payments for drills, services or retaining fees, as earnings. Payments for expenses should\n\
    also be treated as earnings, unless they are wholly, exclusively and necessarily incurred in the\n\
    performance of the duties.\n\n```\n"
